;; What the hyakka growth bots need from a free model, stated as a floor
;; rather than as a name.
;;
;; ADR-2608271450 recorded what a pinned model id costs here: `qwen3.6-35b-a3b`
;; sat in the wiki's ingest config after the fleet stopped serving it, every
;; extraction returned 401 for weeks, and each tick still exited 0. Free models
;; on OpenRouter are strictly more volatile than that — `stealth/ox-alpha` was
;; the most-used model on the platform and served zero endpoints four days
;; later. So no model id is written here as the answer.
;;
;; `resolve_free_model.cljs` reads this, asks OpenRouter which models are free
;; RIGHT NOW, and then *measures* the survivors instead of ranking them by a
;; proxy. Context length is not capability: on 2026-08-27 sorting the free tier
;; by context descending put a 2.6B model above a 550B one.

{;; ---- hard filter, from the models API (no key needed) ------------------
 :min-context 131072   ; the bots read a config, a registry, and receipts
 :require-tools true   ; Hermes drives everything through tool calls

 ;; Routers that pick a free model at random. `openrouter/free` advertises
 ;; itself as exactly that, and a run that draws a 2.6B model cannot fetch a
 ;; URL, write an EDN proposal and open a PR. Excluded by name because the
 ;; models API does not otherwise mark them.
 :exclude-ids #{"openrouter/free" "openrouter/auto"}

 ;; ---- measured, not declared -------------------------------------------
 ;; Probe the top `:probe-count` candidates (context desc) against the three
 ;; things a hyakka bot actually has to do, and install the highest score.
 ;; A candidate that fails the tool call is not probed further.
 :probe-count 6

 ;; ---- credential --------------------------------------------------------
 ;; One place says where the key lives: the login Keychain, read the same way
 ;; the `claude-zai` wrapper in ~/.zshrc reads `gftd.zai`. Rotation is a
 ;; Keychain update and nothing else.
 ;;
 ;; Hermes is handed it through `secrets.command` (a KEY=VALUE helper run once
 ;; per process) rather than `providers.<n>.key_cmd`. key_cmd is the more
 ;; obvious fit and it is what was tried first — the main turn worked, and
 ;; every auxiliary task then failed with
 ;;   'CommandTokenSource' object has no attribute 'strip'
 ;; on hermes v0.20.5. Auxiliary is not only the session title: context
 ;; compression is an auxiliary task, and these bots run 35 minutes.
 :provider-name "openrouter-free"
 :key-env "OPENROUTER_API_KEY"
 :key-read-cmd "security find-generic-password -s gftd.openrouter -w"

 ;; ---- attribution -------------------------------------------------------
 ;; Owner instruction 2026-08-31: OpenRouter traffic from these bots is
 ;; attributed to Itonami, not to the agent that happens to be driving it.
 ;;
 ;; OpenRouter reads the app's identity off two request headers and shows it
 ;; on the app leaderboard and in the account's activity: `HTTP-Referer` (the
 ;; link) and `X-OpenRouter-Title` (the display name; `X-Title` is documented
 ;; as also accepted). Hermes sets BOTH itself and names itself in them —
 ;; `HTTP-Referer: https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com`, `X-Title: Hermes
 ;; Agent`, in `_OR_HEADERS_BASE` (agent/auxiliary_client.py). So this is not
 ;; filling in a blank: without an override, every call this workspace pays
 ;; for is credited upstream.
 ;;
 ;; The resolver writes all three names — the two documented ones plus the
 ;; legacy `X-Title` — because Hermes's default occupies `X-Title`
 ;; specifically. Setting only the new name would leave a stale `Hermes
 ;; Agent` on the wire next to it, and which one OpenRouter prefers when both
 ;; arrive is not ours to assume.
 :attribution {:referer "https://itonami.cloud"
               :title "Itonami By KotobaLabs"}

 ;; ---- how long to wait for one non-streaming call -----------------------
 ;; Hermes arms a stale watchdog on every non-streaming call and kills the
 ;; socket when it fires (`run_agent.py` `_compute_non_stream_stale_timeout`).
 ;; Left implicit it derives 90s, widening to 150s past 50k estimated tokens
 ;; and 240s past 100k — and an implicit budget is additionally capped at half
 ;; the remaining run budget, so it can only ever come DOWN.
 ;;
 ;; Measured 2026-08-30 against ~/.hermes/cron/usage_audit.jsonl: of the 21
 ;; recorded cron errors, 19 are this watchdog firing, and every one of them
 ;; reports an elapsed time equal to one of those three thresholds. The
 ;; scaling is working; the calls are simply longer than it allows. Per-run
 ;; prompt totals ran median 857k / p90 5.1M tokens, and run durations median
 ;; 185s / p90 21min. The bots read a repo and think for 35 minutes; a
 ;; four-minute ceiling on a single large-context turn is below what the work
 ;; costs.
 ;;
 ;; Set here (not as HERMES_API_CALL_STALE_TIMEOUT) so it lands as
 ;; `providers.<n>.stale_timeout_seconds`, which `_stale_timeout_is_explicit`
 ;; treats as user configuration: it becomes the FLOOR the context scaling
 ;; raises from, and it is exempt from the run-budget halving.
 ;;
 ;; 600 rather than the 1800 the upstream example suggests for slow
 ;; large-context turns: a genuinely hung provider still has to be given up
 ;; on inside the 35-minute run, or the watchdog stops being a watchdog.
 :stale-timeout-seconds 600

 ;; ---- response cache ----------------------------------------------------
 ;; Distinct from provider-side prompt caching: this reuses a whole prior
 ;; answer for an identical request. Kept short because a bot's request
 ;; embeds repository and tool state that changes under it, while an exact
 ;; retry inside the window — which is what the fallback chain produces when
 ;; the watchdog fires — should not be paid for twice.
 ;;
 ;; Stated here because it was previously hand-written into config.yaml under
 ;; a top-level `openrouter:` key this resolver did not own. That made the
 ;; resolver REFUSE (exit 2) on every run from that day on, so the daily
 ;; `--if-stale` model re-check silently stopped happening — measured
 ;; 2026-08-30. Owning the key is what lets the resolver run again.
 :response-cache {:enabled true :ttl-seconds 300}

 ;; ---- the floor under the floor ----------------------------------------
 ;; Ours and unbilled. NOT "always up": measured 2026-08-27, the public chat
 ;; API answered 200 at 18:50 and 400 {"error":"Unauthorized"} at 19:05, with
 ;; or without an Authorization header, while `/infer/models/murakumo-main`
 ;; went on reporting `status: serving, verdict: fresh` and `/ready` returned
 ;; 503 (ADR-2608271900). So the resolver measures this at install time
 ;; instead of asserting it, and every free model that also scored 3/3 is
 ;; installed AHEAD of it — a chain of independent free providers first, the
 ;; fleet last.
 :fallback {:provider "custom"
            :model "murakumo-main"
            :base-url "https://api.murakumo.cloud/v1"}

 ;; ---- how often to look again -------------------------------------------
 ;; `--if-stale` is the daily shape: one keyless request to the model list,
 ;; and probing happens only when the installed model has fallen off it or
 ;; the receipt has aged past this. A daily full re-probe would spend ~25
 ;; requests to re-learn the same answer, and would also change the bots'
 ;; model underneath them for no reason.
 :max-receipt-age-days 7

 ;; ---- auxiliary traffic -------------------------------------------------
 ;; Hermes runs side jobs — context compression, session titles — on their
 ;; own chain, and its default OpenRouter fallback is `google/gemini-3.6-flash`,
 ;; which is PAID. Handing Hermes an OpenRouter key without setting this opens
 ;; a billed lane for background traffic on an account with no spend cap.
 ;;
 ;; Two guards, because one is a preference and the other is a ceiling:
 ;;   :tasks     pin the side jobs to the fleet — free, ours, and already
 ;;              proven to serve them (the murakumo sessions have
 ;;              title_generation rows; the free provider had none)
 ;;   :free-only refuse a non-":free" OpenRouter model even if that pin fails
 ;; No per-task pins. Pinning compression and title generation at the fleet
 ;; was tried and every session then emitted
 ;;   ⚠ Auxiliary title generation failed: HTTP 400: {"error":"Unauthorized"}
 ;; because the fleet was down — a pin cannot be more available than what it
 ;; points at. Unpinned, side jobs ride the main provider and use the main
 ;; fallback chain when it rate-limits, which is what actually recovered.
 ;; :free-only stays as the ceiling: never a billed model for background work.
 :auxiliary {:free-only true}

 ;; Owner override. Entries here are TRIED FIRST, in order, and are still
 ;; probed — this reorders the queue, it does not bypass the measurement.
 ;; Leave empty unless you have a reason the probe cannot see.
 ;;
 ;; minimax/minimax-m3:free (2026-08-28 owner request): 1,048,576 context,
 ;; tool-calling, scored 3/3 in the same day's receipt
 ;; (~/.itonami/hermes-free-model/receipt.edn). Probed first; still has to pass.
 :prefer ["minimax/minimax-m3:free"]

 ;; ---- owner override: the primary is BILLED (2026-08-30) ----------------
 ;; This file's premise — bots run on free models — was the owner's, and so
 ;; is this reversal. It is recorded here rather than argued, but the reason
 ;; it was asked for is a measurement, so the measurement is written down.
 ;;
 ;; ~/.hermes/cron/usage_audit.jsonl, 136 cron calls over 2026-08-28..30:
 ;;
 ;;   z-ai/glm-5.3-flash            56 ok   7 fail   11%   median 224s
 ;;   minimax/minimax-m3:free       46 ok  16 fail   26%   median 188s
 ;;   cohere/north-mini-code:free   11 ok   0 fail    0%   median  24s
 ;;
 ;; The free model this policy chose fails at more than twice the rate of the
 ;; billed one, on the bots' own workload, over days rather than one probe.
 ;; Both numbers are from the SAME days and the same jobs, so the comparison
 ;; is not confounded by which bots were running.
 ;;
 ;; Do not read north-mini-code's 0/11 as the best of the three. It is the
 ;; second entry in :fallback-providers, so it is only ever reached after the
 ;; primary has already failed — the 11 calls it served are the short, late
 ;; turns of a retry, not a bot's 35-minute run from the start. Its median of
 ;; 24s against the others' ~200s is the tell. It stays a fallback.
 ;;
 ;; Cost, measured the same day from OpenRouter's /key endpoint: $0.14/day,
 ;; $3.38 lifetime. The account has NO spend cap (`limit: null`), which is
 ;; the hazard the :auxiliary note below was written about — a cap belongs on
 ;; the key regardless of which model is primary.
 ;;
 ;; :auxiliary :free-only stays true. Side jobs are background traffic and
 ;; nothing measured here says they should be billed too.
 ;;
 ;; What the resolver still does with this set: it checks every run that the
 ;; id is still on OpenRouter's model list, and exits 1 saying so when it is
 ;; not. That is the one guard ADR-2608271450 asked for — a pinned id that
 ;; has stopped existing must not present as a healthy tick.
 ;; ---- owner override: the primary is THE FLEET (2026-09-09) -------------
 ;; Superseding the 2026-08-30 billed override above, which stays in place as
 ;; the record of why the free tier was left. Owner instruction: run the bots
 ;; on murakumo.cloud's Qwen 27B.
 ;;
 ;; The 2026-08-27 note further up says the fleet is "ours and unbilled, NOT
 ;; always up", and installs it last for that reason. What changed is not the
 ;; argument, it is the measurement. `api.murakumo.cloud` was answering all
 ;; day on 2026-09-09, and murakumo-main scored 3/3 on the SAME three probes
 ;; this file measures the free tier with:
 ;;
 ;;   tools  PASS  one record_source call, exact url and license
 ;;   exact  PASS  replied HYAKKA-OK and nothing else
 ;;   edn    PASS  {:id "a" :ctx 7}
 ;;
 ;; A `:base-url` here means the primary is NOT an OpenRouter id, so the
 ;; resolver's usual guard — is the id still on OpenRouter's list — cannot
 ;; run and would pass vacuously if it did. It probes this endpoint with the
 ;; three tasks instead. That substitution is the whole reason a fleet primary
 ;; is safe to pin: the guard still measures something, and it measures the
 ;; thing that would actually break.
 ;;
 ;; The free chain does not go away. It moves under this one, walked when the
 ;; fleet rate-limits or goes down — which is the arrangement the 2026-08-27
 ;; note asked for, with the two ends swapped.
 ;; What the fleet displaced. It stays in the chain as the first fallback,
 ;; because a primary with an empty chain under it is worse than the
 ;; arrangement it replaced: the free tier answers 429 all afternoon but it
 ;; answers, and on the first run of this change the probe found no free model
 ;; that scored 3/3, so `fallback_providers` came out EMPTY. The claim in the
 ;; note below -- that the free chain moves under the fleet -- has to be
 ;; something the renderer does, not something the comment says.
 :displaced-primary {:provider "openrouter-free"
                     :model "z-ai/glm-5.3-flash"
                     :measured-at "2026-08-30"}

 ;; `:context-length` is not optional for a non-OpenRouter primary. Hermes
 ;; resolves a model's window from the provider's model list, gets nothing for
 ;; this endpoint, and falls through to a value that made the first live run
 ;; die with `Context length exceeded (32 tokens). Cannot compress further.` --
 ;; on a 32-token prompt. Every one of the 58 cron jobs would have failed that
 ;; way. 262,144 is what api.murakumo.cloud publishes for murakumo-main in
 ;; `/v1/models`; the pool's b70 member has a smaller per-slot window and the
 ;; gateway routes around it, which is the gateway's contract to keep, not the
 ;; bots'.
 :primary {:provider "murakumo"
           :model "murakumo-main"
           :base-url "https://api.murakumo.cloud/v1"
           :context-length 262144
           :billed false
           :measured-at "2026-09-09"
           :evidence "3/3 on the same probe the free tier is measured with"}}
